Context: Users and downstream SDKs reported friction with the existing stream APIs and auth handshake coupling (token exchange logic embedded in transport layers). This PR introduces a coordinated cross-SDK change: 1) rename the streaming surface from `stream()` to `subscribe()` to better reflect push/update semantics and to align ergonomics across Python/TypeScript/Go SDKs, 2) extract an explicit CredentialProvider interface that decouples token lifecycle (refresh, preflight, lease) from transport, enabling safer retries and clearer ownership, 3) introduce small retry-profile presets tuned for streaming (latency-first, throughput-first) and expose them consistently across SDKs, and 4) bump package versions and update docs and examples. This PR is intended to be backward-compatible via a deprecation shim: existing `stream()` calls still work but emit a deprecation warning and delegate to the new subscribe facade. Motivation: Having both API and auth semantics diverge across languages increased cognitive load for integrators and made cross-language examples error-prone. By defining a minimal CredentialProvider contract (get_token, refresh_if_needed, lease_ttl_hint) and an ergonomic subscribe async iterator / async generator / channel abstraction per-language, we simplify patterns for reconnects, token rotation, and deterministic shutdown. Checklist: - [x] API design review and signature lock (ENG-4321) - [x] TS implementation + unit/integration tests - [x] Py implementation + unit/integration tests - [x] Go implementation + unit/integration tests - [x] Docs + examples updated - [x] Backwards-compat shim and deprecation notice - [x] Release notes drafted Summary of commits: - Extract credential provider abstraction and small test harness - Implement subscribe facade in TypeScript (async generator + convenience helper) - Implement subscribe facade in Python (async iterator + context manager) - Implement subscribe facade in Go (channel-based subscriber + Close semantics) - Add retry profile presets and wire to subscribe plumbing - Add deprecation shim for legacy stream() callsites and tests - Docs: streaming.md rewrite + examples/subscribe demos - Changelog bumps: py 1.31.0, ts 1.27.0, go 1.8.1 High-level changed areas/files (representative, not exhaustive): - sdk/typescript/src/subscribe/index.ts (new facade + docs) - sdk/typescript/src/auth/credential_provider.ts (new interface + tests) - sdk/python/redwood/subscribe.py (new facade + examples) - sdk/python/redwood/auth/credential_provider.py (new abstraction + tests) - sdk/go/subscriber/subscribe.go (new channel-based API) - sdk/go/cred/provider.go (new credential provider contract) - docs/streaming.md (rewritten to show subscribe + credential provider patterns) - examples/subscribe/ (updated multi-lang examples) - changelogs/python/1.31.0.md, changelogs/ts/1.27.0.md, changelogs/go/1.8.1.md Small illustrative snippet (TS): ```ts
// new subscribe convenience helper
for await (const msg of client.subscribe({route: 'chat'}) ) {
  // handle chunked events
}
``` Compatibility and migration notes: - `client.stream()` will continue to work but logs a deprecation warning; callsites should migrate to `client.subscribe()` before the next major version. - The new CredentialProvider interface is intentionally tiny; internal transport adapters provide a default provider that mirrors previous behavior. - No customer-facing server changes required; this is an SDK surface change only. Risk and mitigation: - Risk: Regressions in streaming reconnect/reauth paths. Mitigation: added integration tests that exercise token rotation mid-stream and a small canary to exercise long-running subscriptions during rollout. - Risk: Type/shape drift between SDKs. Mitigation: cross-language API contract doc and test vectors included. Rollout plan: - Merge to main, publish minor releases for each SDK in the same deployment window, monitor canary usage, follow up with deprecation schedule in docs and a support banner.
Liam O'Connor (reviewer): Overall looks good, I have two questions: 1) can we keep the previous transport-level preflight check for auth to avoid an extra roundtrip in the common case? 2) how does this interact with dedicated/delegated auth tokens?
Asha Patel (author): For (1) we kept a transport preflight path in the default provider — it short-circuits when cached_token.valid() is true, so no extra roundtrip. For (2) the CredentialProvider supports a lease_ttl_hint so callers (Dedicated/Enterprise) can plug in leased tokens; added notes in docs and tests that simulate short TTLs.
Priya Nair (reviewer): Please add a small test that simulates token rotation mid-stream and assert no duplicate messages nor silent stream terminations. Also add a TS typing note for the async generator return type.
Asha Patel (author): Added integration test test_subscribe_token_rotation in both TS and Py; TS typing updated to AsyncIterable<Message | ControlEvent>.
Ethan Brooks (reviewer): Minor nit: rename subscribe.close() -> close() on the subscriber for consistency with Go's Close() pattern? We should standardize names rather than having language-specific variants.
Asha Patel (author): Renamed the Python/TS convenience helper `subscriber.close()` to `close()` and added adapter notes. Kept Go's `Close()` idiomatic casing but documented the mapping.
Cross-SDK: introduce `subscribe()` facade (deprecates `stream()`), add CredentialProvider abstraction to decouple token lifecycle from transport, introduce streaming retry presets, bump versions: Python 1.31.0, TypeScript 1.27.0, Go 1.8.1. Backwards-compatible shim included; migration guide in docs/streaming.md.

Motivation: In high-throughput multi-tenant workloads we observed tail-latency regressions caused by eviction work items being scheduled without regard to request-criticality and small-fragmented allocations causing repeated spill/rehydration churn. This PR introduces a latency-aware eviction scheduler and a compact-hint bitset per shard to drive low-cost background micro-compactions. The goal is to reduce tail latency and spill I/O while keeping steady-state memory residency high.\n\nSummary of changes: 1) eviction/scheduler: add a two-queue eviction scheduler (latency-critical queue + background queue). Items touched by recent user-facing requests are scored with a higher \"latency priority\" factor so eviction work that could block an in-flight request is deferred or scheduled with a bounded preemption window. 2) compact-hint bitset: each KV shard maintains a compact-hint bitset (one bit per slot-range) toggled by allocation/fragmentation heuristics. Background micro-compact workers sample the bitset and perform cheap in-place coalescing where useful. 3) async micro-compaction: lightweight non-blocking compaction paths that operate on cold ranges and return a compacted index without pausing the hot path; compaction work interacts with the scheduler so heavy compaction is rate-limited during request spikes. 4) spill prioritizer: improve spill ordering by introducing a size-weighted stale-score that favors evicting large cold allocations first to reduce immediate spill-backpressure. 5) kernel/scheduler knobs: exposed runtime knobs to configure latency threshold, max background compaction bandwidth, and scheduler preemption window for canary tuning.\n\nFiles changed (high level): serving/kv/cache/evictor.rs - new scheduler and scoring heuristics; serving/kv/cache/compact_hint.rs - bitset and sampling; serving/kv/cache/async_compactor.rs - async compaction worker and protocols; serving/kv/cache/pager.go - spill prioritizer changes used by pager; bench/perf/kv_eviction_bench.py - synthetic bench harness for multi-tenant eviction latencies; docs/ops/kv-eviction.md - operational guidance and knobs; tests/kv/test_eviction_policy.py - unit+integration tests.\n\nDesign notes and rationale: - Latency-aware scheduling: Eviction previously used a single priority queue based mostly on LRU+age which caused eviction work to preempt CPU during tail request handling (e.g., refresh/rehydrate). We limit eviction interference by tracking a per-request touch bit and enforcing a short \"grace period\" where touched items are either pinned or moved to background queue. The scheduler also supports a bounded preemption window so urgent eviction tasks aren't starved but won't spike tail latency. - Compact-hint bitset: Full compaction passes were expensive and caused allocation stall. A lightweight bitset flags fragmented regions; background workers probe hotness metrics and only compact if expected free-coalesced bytes exceed a threshold. This reduces unnecessary work and targets compaction where fragmentation yields net benefit. - Async micro-compaction: Implemented using compare-and-swap friendly metadata updates to avoid global locks. The compaction produces a small rewrite plan applied atomically to index pointers; large moves offloaded to spill pipeline. - Spill prioritizer: Reorders candidate pages to prefer large, cold ranges (size-weighted stale score) which reduces number of downstream I/O operations and rehydrate churn for common request patterns.\n\nTesting and results: - Unit tests cover scoring edge cases, bitset toggling, and atomic commit of micro-compaction plans. - Integration tests in tests/kv/test_eviction_policy.py simulate tenant mix (hot/cold) and verify correctness under concurrent access. - Perf bench: bench/perf/kv_eviction_bench.py ran 30-minute experiments comparing main vs this branch under a 60:40 hot/cold tenant mix. Observed 95th-tail latency reduction of 18-28% across GPU-backed inference workloads and a 22% reduction in total spill bytes under sustained load. Memory residency improved slightly (~3%) due to smarter compaction targeting. Detailed numbers included in the PR comment thread.\n\nRollout plan and safety: - Feature guarded behind runtime flags (env + console toggle). - Canary rollout to 10% of serving clusters for 48h with increased observability (new metrics: eviction_queue_length, compact_hint_activity, spill_priority_histogram). - Automatic rollback if 95th-tail latency regresses >10% or spill bytes increase >15%.\n\nRelated: ENG-1849 (eviction scheduling), ENG-2202 (kv-compaction roadmap), SUP-7781 (customer incident triggered further prioritization).\n\nNotes for reviewers: focus review on atomic commit logic in async_compactor.rs and scheduler preemption semantics in evictor.rs. The pager.go changes adjust spill selection but preserve I/O batching behavior; reviewer input on heuristics is welcome.\n\nMerge: squash merge after approval and green CI.
--- a/serving/kv/cache/evictor.rs
+++ b/serving/kv/cache/evictor.rs
@@ -120,6 +120,22 @@ impl Evictor {
     fn schedule(&self, candidate: EvictCandidate) {
-        self.queue.push(candidate);
+        // latency-aware placement: prefer background queue if touched recently
+        if candidate.touched_within(self.config.latency_grace_ms) {
+            self.background_queue.push(candidate);
+        } else {
+            self.latency_queue.push(candidate.with_score(self.score(&candidate)));
+        }
     }
+
+    fn score(&self, c: &EvictCandidate) -> f64 {
+        // combine age, size and fragmentation penalty into a single priority
+        (c.age_ms as f64 * self.config.age_weight) + (c.size_bytes as f64 * self.config.size_weight) + (c.fragmentation as f64 * self.config.frag_penalty)
+    }
